A new channel is a new class implementing the same interface, registered once — the dispatcher's code never changes.

```python
class ChannelSender(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def send(self, notification, user) -> DeliveryStatus: ...

class WhatsAppChannel(ChannelSender):
    def __init__(self, client, template_renderer):
        self.client = client
        self.template_renderer = template_renderer

    def send(self, notification, user) -> DeliveryStatus:
        if not user.preferences.allows("whatsapp"):
            return DeliveryStatus.SKIPPED
        message = self.template_renderer.render(notification, "whatsapp")
        return self.client.send_message(user.phone, message)

dispatcher.register(WhatsAppChannel(whatsapp_client, renderer))
```

`Dispatcher.dispatch()` only ever loops over registered `ChannelSender`s calling `send()` — it has no `WhatsAppChannel`-specific code path, satisfying open/closed: open for a new channel, closed to modifying the dispatcher.
